window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"18-06","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"18-06-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-01/","title_he":"א – חזקת טהרה","title_en":"The Presumption of Purity","body_en":"<p>Every woman who has become pure through a proper tevila (immersion) is under a presumption of purity for her husband, and she may have union with her husband without any bedika (internal examination) and without concern that perhaps she has seen blood. On the contrary, the Sages said (Nidda 12a) that it is preferable that she not examine herself before union, so that her husband's heart not trouble him – that perhaps his wife is examining because she felt that her veset (menstrual period) had begun, and did not see blood in her bedika because she did not examine well, or because the blood dissolved and was lost, or because the bleeding has not yet begun but will begin during the union. After union too it is preferable that she not examine, because it is not fitting to raise doubts about the mitzva of ona (the marital mitzva) that they have already fulfilled, lest their hearts trouble them in the future so that they become apprehensive about fulfilling the mitzva. This is what the Sages said (Nidda 15a): \"All women are under a presumption of purity for their husbands,\" and even \"those who come back from a journey – their wives are under a presumption of purity for them.\" The husband should not be concerned that perhaps, while he was far from home, his wife did not attend to her purity and perhaps saw blood without being aware of it; therefore, even when she is asleep, he may come and lie beside her. But at the ona (time period) of her veset, that is, at the time when there is a reasonable concern that she will get her veset, they must refrain from union, as will be explained in the following halakhot.<sup>1</sup></p><p>However, not close to union and unconnected with it, it is a virtue for a woman to examine herself frequently, because at times it happens that the veset arrives at an unexpected time, and through these bedikot she will know of it (Nidda 13a). In the past, when means of wiping were less available, and there were no toilet rooms in which one could relieve oneself comfortably and in privacy, only women who were meticulous about their cleanliness and purity examined themselves every time they relieved themselves. But nowadays almost all women are meticulous in this, in that they look at the panty liner, or at the toilet paper with which they wiped after relieving themselves in the toilet, and through this they know whether the veset has begun. However, a woman who is liable to see ketamim (bloodstains; singular ketem) during her days of purity, because of wounds she has, or because of an intrauterine device or hormonal pills or for other reasons, would do better not to look at the toilet paper at the beginning of wiping, so that her heart not trouble her over ketamim. For the purpose of these bedikot is to know whether an unexpected veset has arrived, and not to check whether there are ketamim.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"In Nidda 11a the Mishna explains that a woman must examine herself before union. And the Gemara there (11b) explained: \"Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: They taught this only with regard to taharot (ritually pure foods), but for her husband she is permitted\" without a bedika. That is, when a woman had to examine herself morning and evening for taharot, they required her to examine also before union; but when she does not examine herself for taharot, she need not examine before union. Further on in the Gemara there (12a): \"Rabbi Zeira asked Rav Yehuda: May a woman examine herself for her husband (as a measure of piety, before union)? He said to him: She should not examine. But let her examine – what harm is there? If so, his heart would trouble him and he would refrain.\" And so answered Rav Huna, that for this reason one should also not examine after union. And so ruled most Rishonim: Rashi, Tosafot, Ritva, Semag, Teruma, Rokeaḥ, Mordekhai in the name of the Rashbam, Ra'avad and others. In their view this is the law both for a woman who has a fixed veset and for a woman who does not have a fixed veset.<br>On the other hand, some learn from the continuation of the Gemara (12b) that the halakha is like Rabbi Ḥanina ben Antigonus, that even for her husband she must examine herself; but they disagreed about the interpretation of his words. According to Rabbeinu Ḥananel, a woman who has no fixed veset must always examine before union and after it. The Rosh took his view into account, and so ruled the Rambam, and the SA (YD 186:2) cited their view as a second opinion (and Torat Ha-shelamim 2 and Ḥavot Da'at 2 even understood that the SA holds so in practice). And according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Issurei Bi'ah 4:16), even a woman who has a fixed veset must examine herself after relations. His view was cited in his name in SA 186:1. And according to the Rif, Ramban and Rashba, the intent is that a bride who has no fixed veset must, after her wedding, examine herself on the first three occasions, to verify that she does not see on account of relations.<br>In practice, since according to most Rishonim one should refrain from bedikot lest his heart trouble him, one should not be stringent like the view of those who require a bedika, for this stringency is a leniency with regard to the mitzva of ona (see SA and Rema 186:1; Rema 184:1). Even if the husband wishes to be stringent and to ask his wife to examine, it is proper that he refrain from this, so that they not arouse anxieties for themselves in this matter. So wrote Sidrei Tahara 190:67, and Pardes Rimonim 186, Siftei Ḥakhamim [?] 1. This is unlike the view of Pleti 186:1 and Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 184:3, who held that if the husband asks for the bedika, there is no concern that his heart will trouble him. However, regarding the view of the Rif, some rule like him, as explained below 8:16."},{"n":2,"en":"Nidda 13a, in the Mishna: \"Every hand that examines frequently is, among women, praiseworthy.\" The Rashba wrote (Torat Ha-bayit, Bayit 7, Sha'ar 2) that the praiseworthy bedikot are \"specifically at a time when she is not about to attend to her household [to have union]… but at a time when she is about to attend to her household – she does not examine.\" It suffices to perform these hiddur (enhanced observance) bedikot by external bedikot, in the manner of wiping in the toilet. And although according to most Rishonim ordinary bedikot are inside the vagina and wiping alone does not suffice (above chapter 4, note 4), it seems that for these hiddur bedikot, which are intended to know whether the veset has begun, they would agree that a bedika by wiping suffices (and so wrote Ḥavot Da'at 186, Bi'urim 1; Sidrei Tahara 184:13; Pri De'ah 186:1, regarding the bedikot before and after relations on the first three occasions, as explained below 8:16). On the contrary, since the purpose of the hiddur bedikot is to check whether the veset has arrived, it is preferable to perform them by wiping, in such a way that if there are ketamim they will not render her impure. And since wiping in the toilet is with paper, which is not susceptible to impurity, if blood is found on it, it does not render impure under the law of ketem (above 2:9). It emerges that on the one hand this includes the hiddur of a bedika to know whether the veset has begun, and on the other hand, if there is a ketem, it will not render her impure."}]},{"slug":"18-06-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-02/","title_he":"ב – מצוות הפרישה בעונת חשש הווסת","title_en":"The Mitzva of Separation at the Time of Concern for the Veset","body_en":"<p>The Sages said (Shevu'ot 18b) that there is a mitzva for the couple to refrain from union during the ona in which the woman is liable to get her veset, as it is stated (Vayikra 15:31): \"Thus you shall keep apart (that is, separate) the children of Israel from their impurity, so that they not die in their impurity when they defile My Tabernacle that is in their midst.\" Therefore the Sages instituted that when the ona arrives in which the woman is liable to see her veset, the couple should refrain from union, lest the veset begin during the union. An ona is a day or a night (as explained below, section 4).</p><p>A few verses after the mitzva to separate Israel from their impurity, it is stated (Vayikra 16:1): \"And the Lord spoke to Moshe after the death of the two sons of Aharon, when they drew near before the Lord and died,\" to teach us that even a great and righteous person may be punished if he is not careful to withdraw from holiness at a time when this is necessary (see Shevu'ot 18b). The intention of Aharon's sons was good – they wished to draw close to God – but they breached the boundary and died. As it is stated (Vayikra 10:1-2): \"And the sons of Aharon, Nadav and Avihu, each took his fire pan, and put fire in it, and laid incense on it, and offered strange fire before the Lord, which He had not commanded them. And fire went out from before the Lord and consumed them, and they died before the Lord.\" Similarly, the mitzva of ona is a mitzva through which the Shekhina (Divine Presence) dwells (Sota 17a; Ramban in Iggeret Ha-kodesh). But when it is done without care to avoid the prohibition, it renders them impure and removes the Shekhina from among them. And concerning this the Torah warned: \"Thus you shall keep apart (that is, separate) the children of Israel from their impurity, so that they not die in their impurity when they defile <b>My Tabernacle</b> that is in their midst.\"</p><p>A couple who sanctify themselves in the mitzva of ona, that is, who fulfill the mitzva with joy, out of faithfulness and love, and at the same time are careful to separate at the ona of the veset – will merit children who know how to restrain themselves and to add goodness and blessing. Their sons will be men of understanding, responsible and diligent, and fit to give rulings in Torah. And their daughters will be women of insight, fit to absorb the word of God and to reveal it throughout the expanses of life (based on Shevu'ot 18b).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Shevu'ot 18b: \"'Thus you shall keep apart the children of Israel from their impurity' – Rabbi Yoshiya said: From here is a warning to the children of Israel that they separate from their wives close to their veset.\" According to most Rishonim and Aḥaronim, the warning to separate is rabbinic, and the verse is an asmakhta (a scriptural support). So wrote the Ra'avad, Semag, Rashba, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Mordekhai, Shakh, Taz, Tiferet Le-Moshe and others. According to Rabbeinu Tam and the Yere'im, the warning is from the Torah (and so wrote the Noda Bi-Yehuda, Kamma YD 55, and the Ḥatam Sofer YD 170, based on several Rishonim, that the obligation of separation is from the Torah; but if the ona of the veset has passed and she did not see and did not feel, the concern that she saw is only rabbinic. As explained below in note 5, the halakha is like the Sages and Rav that vestot are rabbinic, meaning that if she did not examine herself at the end of the ona and did not feel that her veset had begun, she is not considered impure. The Ḥatam Sofer 179 added that for a veset that is not fixed, all agree that the separation is rabbinic).<br>It is further stated in Shevu'ot there: \"Rabbi Yoḥanan said in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yoḥai: Whoever does not separate from his wife close to her veset, even if he has sons like the sons of Aharon, they die, as it is written (Vayikra 15:31-33): 'Thus you shall keep apart the children of Israel from their impurity… and of her who is unwell in her impurity,' and adjacent to it (ibid. 16:1): 'After the death.' Rabbi Ḥiya bar Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yoḥanan: Whoever separates from his wife close to her veset will have male children, as it is written (ibid. 11:47): 'To distinguish between the impure and the pure,' and adjacent to it (ibid. 12:2): 'If a woman conceives and bears a male child.' Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: He will have sons fit to give halakhic rulings, as it is written: 'To distinguish between the impure and the pure…' Rabbi Binyamin bar Yefet said in the name of Rabbi Elazar: Whoever sanctifies himself at the time of relations will have male children, as it is stated (ibid. 11:44): 'Sanctify yourselves and be holy,' and adjacent to it (ibid. 12:2): 'If a woman conceives and bears a male child.'\" In Peninei Halakha: Simḥat Ha-bayit U-virkhato 1:1-2; 3:3; 10, it is explained that holiness is the fulfillment of the mitzva of ona with abundant joy, out of faithfulness and love.<br>The Maharal explained in Ḥiddushei Aggadot on Shevu'ot there that meriting male children means that the sons will have the special qualities that males have: men of understanding, responsible and diligent, and fit to give rulings in Torah. End quote. Accordingly, since after explaining the law of a woman who gives birth to a male the Torah goes on to explain the law of a woman who gives birth to a female – \"But if she bears a female\" – it follows that through the care to separate close to the veset and to sanctify oneself for the mitzva, one merits daughters as well, for the blessing for women always continues after the blessing of the sons. And the more the talents of women are revealed, the more we must deepen our explanation of the continuation of this blessing. If so, the intent is that by virtue of the separation and the care, they will merit daughters who will reveal the special qualities that daughters have, with abundant insight and in revealing the word of God throughout all the expanses of life, as I wrote above."}]},{"slug":"18-06-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-03/","title_he":"ג – דיני הפרישה","title_en":"The Laws of Separation","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of separation is to refrain from union, but hugging and kissing out of affection are permitted, because the Sages forbade only union, lest the veset begin during the union. However, a couple who are concerned that hugging might lead them to the prohibition of union, or that the husband might come to emit seed in vain, must refrain from hugging as well.<sup>4</sup></p><p>Toward the end of the ona the woman must examine herself with an internal bedika in the vagina, to know whether the veset has begun. In a night ona she should examine herself after she gets up (the manner of the bedika is explained above 4:3). And if she did not examine herself at the end of the ona, after a fixed veset or an ona beinonit (the \"average ona\", explained below in section 5), they should not have union until she examines and sees that she is clean (SA YD 189:4).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"According to the Ra'avad, Ramban, Rashba, SA 184:2, Rema 184:10 and many others, the obligation of separation is only from union. On the other hand, according to Terumat Ha-deshen 250, hugging and kissing are also forbidden during the ona of separation, since according to the Rambam and most Rishonim they are included in the intimacy forbidden by the Torah. So too ruled the Taz 3, Leḥem Ve-simla 4, Ben Ish Ḥai, Tzav 2. However, since according to halakha the obligation of separation is rabbinic, the halakha follows the view of most poskim that only union, whose punishment is karet, was forbidden by the safeguard of separation, and not hugging and kissing, which even during the days of prohibition are forbidden by the Torah with a negative commandment and not with karet. In addition, the Baḥ explained that union is forbidden lest it cause the appearance of the veset, and consequently there is no reason to forbid hugging. The Responsa of the Radbaz (1:163) wrote on this law: \"I do not see fit to innovate stringencies upon Israel that the earlier authorities were not stringent about; would that they keep what has been imposed upon them, for if you grasp too much you have not grasped, and they will be left with neither this nor that.\" However, when there is a concern that affectionate contact will lead them to the prohibition of union, it is clear that they must refrain from it, and so wrote Darkhei Tahara 6:14.<br>Since in practice only union is forbidden, some permit sleeping together without clothing (Shulḥan Gavoha 5; Ziv'ḥei Tzedek 8). However, it is proper to refrain from this, and only with clothing may those who wish to be lenient do so. And it is good to be stringent not to sleep embracing even with clothing, lest the veset begin during their sleep (see Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Kuntres Aḥaron 1; Taharat Ha-bayit 2:2, p. 72)."},{"n":5,"en":"In Nidda 16a it is explained that a woman must examine at the ona of her veset whether she has begun to see, and Tanna'im and Amora'im disagreed whether vestot – that is, the concern that she will see blood at the ona of the veset – are from the Torah or rabbinic. According to R. Meir and Shmuel, the concern for vestot is from the Torah, like the law of any ḥazaka (presumption); consequently, if she did not examine herself immediately at the end of her ona, even if she examines afterwards and does not see blood, she is under a presumption of impurity, for the presumption is that the bleeding came at its time and apparently was not found because it was lost. However, according to the Sages and Rav, the concern for vestot is rabbinic; that is, there is no presumption that blood came out, but the Sages merely instituted that she separate during that ona and examine. And so is the halakha, that vestot are rabbinic. Therefore, even if she examined herself after a long time, as long as she did not feel and did not see blood she is still under a presumption of purity. According to the Rif, she is even permitted in relations without a bedika. However, according to Tosafot and the Rashba, she is forbidden until she examines. And although it was ruled that vestot are rabbinic, since it is within her power to examine, one should be stringent that if she did not examine at the end of the ona, they should not have relations without a bedika. And so it was ruled in SA 189:4 and Rema 184:9. (However, the Re'em, Teruma and Mordekhai are stringent that if she did not examine immediately at the end of her ona, she is under a presumption of impurity and a bedika will no longer help her, and the Baḥ and Shakh took their view into account. But the rest of the poskim did not take their stringency into account, and so wrote the Beit Yosef, Taz, Pleti, Ḥavot Da'at and many others. See above, note 3, that according to most poskim the separation at the ona of the veset is rabbinic, and some say it is from the Torah.)<br>It suffices to examine once at the end of the ona. (Some say that le-khatḥila she should also examine at its beginning: Levushei Oz 9, Tamtzit 8; Badei Ha-shulḥan 54; Shi'urei Shevet Ha-levi, Summary 184:9:1.) The Ḥavot Da'at 184:9 was stringent to require placing a mokh daḥuk (a firmly inserted cloth) throughout the entire ona, and many disagreed with him (see Imrei Bina 6; Erekh Shai; Maharash Engel 4:49). Several Aḥaronim wrote that in recent generations, when women began to wear underwear, a woman who wishes to be meticulous like the Ḥavot Da'at need not place a mokh daḥuk, but it suffices that she wear white underwear, for since they fit closely to the body they absorb any blood like a mokh daḥuk; and at the end of the ona she should examine herself with an internal bedika (Keren Le-David YD 178; Igrot Moshe YD 3:48).<br>Also a woman who has no fixed veset, who must separate out of doubt for three onot (as explained in section 5), must examine herself in each of the three onot in which she separates, toward its end. And if she did not examine: after an ona beinonit she is forbidden in relations until she examines, and after the onat ha-haflaga and the onat ha-ḥodesh she need not examine (SA 189:4)."}]},{"slug":"18-06-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-04/","title_he":"ד – משך עונת הפרישה","title_en":"The Duration of the Ona of Separation","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of separation is during the 'ona' in which it is likely that the woman will get her veset. Women who have a veset kavu'a (fixed veset), that is, whose cycle has appeared precisely three times in succession, must separate during the ona in which the veset is due to appear (as explained in section 6). And women who do not have a fixed veset must separate during the three onot in which there is a greater chance that the veset may appear (as explained in the next section).</p><p>An 'ona' is a day or a night. The day ona is from sunrise until sunset, and the night ona from sunset until sunrise. If the previous veset began by day – the separation must be in a day ona; if the previous veset began at night – the separation must be in a night ona. Even a woman who usually gets her veset at the beginning of the ona or at its end must separate throughout the entire ona, but in the ona before it or after it she need not separate. And although some hold that le-khatḥila it is good to be stringent and separate also one ona before, the halakha follows the view of the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim, that one must separate for one ona only.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In the Mishna, Nidda 63b: according to R. Yossi, if within the ona there is a specific hour at which the veset usually comes, one must separate only at that hour, and according to R. Yehuda one must separate throughout that entire ona. And Rava said that the halakha is like Rabbi Yehuda. And so is the view of all the Rishonim, among them: Rif, Rambam, Ra'avad, Rashba, Semag, Semak and others, and so it was ruled in SA YD 184:2. (However, a woman who has no fixed veset but always sees at the same hour of the day – in the three onot for which she is concerned, she need be concerned only at that hour at which she always usually sees, as explained in SA 189:3.)<br>However, the 'Or Zaru'a' (1:358) wrote in the name of his teacher the Ra'aviya that it is customary to separate also one ona before. The Baḥ, Shakh 184:7, Ḥatam Sofer, Ben Ish Ḥai, Tzav 1, and others took his words into account. However, according to most Aḥaronim one need not be concerned for this, among them: Taz, Torat Ha-shelamim, Sidrei Tahara, Pleti, Ḥavot Da'at 3, Maharsham, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 20, Taharat Ha-bayit 2:1.<br>The ona is a day or a night, with the change of onot at sunrise and at sunset (SA 184:4-5), unlike the mitzvot of the day, for which in a pressing situation the day is reckoned from dawn (Megilla 20b). In winter the day ona is short and the night ona long, while in summer the reverse. However, the Ra'aviya wrote in his book Avi'asaf that an ona is a day or a night as in the days of Nisan and Tishrei, when the length of the day and of the night is equal, that is, twelve hours. Consequently, in the days of Tamuz and Tevet one must take care that the ona be twelve hours. However, the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim did not take his view into account. In addition, it is not clear how these twelve hours are to be calculated, and it is not possible that the Sages would establish a halakha whose fulfillment is difficult to calculate."}]},{"slug":"18-06-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-05/","title_he":"ה – הפרישה לווסת שאינו קבוע","title_en":"Separation for a Veset That Is Not Fixed","body_en":"<p>A woman who does not have a fixed veset must separate during the three onot in which there is a greater chance that her veset will begin: a) onat ha-haflaga (the ona of the interval); b) ona beinonit (the average ona); c) onat ha-ḥodesh (the monthly ona). These three onot are calculated from the last veset, but the one before it is not taken into account (SA YD 189:1; Rema 13). At times one must also be concerned for veset ha-guf (a bodily veset), that is, distinct physical signs that appear before the veset, as explained below (section 7).</p><p>Explanation of the three onot: a) Onat ha-haflaga, that is, the interval between the last veset and the one that preceded it, because at times the veset appears in a cycle of intervals. For example, if thirty-five days passed between the last cycle and the one that preceded it, there is a chance that so it will be the next time as well. Therefore one should count thirty-five days from the previous cycle and observe the ona of separation of the haflaga. b) Ona beinonit, that is, thirty days after the previous veset, because it is common for the veset to appear in a thirty-day cycle. In the count of thirty days, the day of the previous seeing is counted as the first day, and the day on which one must separate as the thirtieth day. In practice the ona beinonit always falls after four weeks and one day. For if the previous veset was on Sunday, in the day ona, after four weeks and a day, that is, on Monday in the day ona, the time of the ona beinonit will arrive. c) Onat ha-ḥodesh, that is, on the same day and ona in the following month, because at times the veset appears according to the cycle of the moon, by which the Hebrew month is determined. For example, if in the previous month the veset appeared on the 3rd of the month, one must separate in the following month on the 3rd of the month. It should be noted that when the previous month was ḥaser (29 days), the 'onat ha-ḥodesh' and the 'ona beinonit' will fall in the same ona. When the previous month was malei (30 days), the 'onat ha-ḥodesh' will come one day after the 'ona beinonit'.</p><p>As we have learned, an ona is a day or a night; it emerges that if the previous veset began by day, as happens many times, all three onot in which one must separate fall in the day ona. If the previous veset began at night, all three onot fall in the night ona.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"18-06-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-06/","title_he":"ו – הפרישה לווסת קבוע","title_en":"Separation for a Fixed Veset","body_en":"<p>For a woman who has a fixed veset, the ona of separation is one ona only, in which she is due to get the next veset. A fixed veset is established by its appearance in a fixed cycle three times in succession. There are various kinds of fixity: a 'veset ha-haflaga' (interval veset) is established after there was an equal interval between the vestot three times in succession, for example after thirty-five days in the day ona; from then on they must always separate thirty-five days after the previous veset, in the day ona. A 'veset ha-ḥodesh' (monthly veset) is established after the veset appeared three times in succession on the same date of the Hebrew month and in the same ona; from then on they must separate every month on that date and in that ona. And so too anything similar: whenever the veset appeared three times in a fixed cycle, the woman has established a veset for herself, and from then on they must separate only at the ona of her fixed veset.</p><p>A woman who had a fixed veset and changed, and saw not at the time of her fixed veset, should the next time be concerned for three onot: a) for the fixed veset; b) for the onat ha-ḥodesh from the last seeing; c) for the onat ha-haflaga from the last seeing. But she is not concerned for the ona beinonit, since the law of separation at the ona beinonit exists only for a woman who has no fixed veset. And if three times in succession she sees not in accordance with her fixed pattern, her fixed veset is cancelled, and her law reverts to the law of a woman who has no fixed veset.<sup>7</sup></p><p>At times a woman has a 'semi-fixed' veset, according to which she does not know on which day she will see, but she does know that the length of her cycle is at most twenty-nine days, or at least thirty-two days. Then she must be concerned only for the calculation of the previous haflaga, and not for the 'ona beinonit' or the 'onat ha-ḥodesh', since these onot fall on days on which she knows she will not see. A 'semi-fixed' veset is established on the basis of three consecutive cycles.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"If afterwards she again sees, even once, according to the previous fixed veset that she had, since in the meantime she has not established another fixed veset, she reverts to her previous fixed veset, and only after she does not see at it three times in succession is it uprooted again. And if in the meantime she established another fixed veset, she no longer takes her previous fixed veset into account, and only if she again sees at it three times in succession does she establish it anew (SA YD 189:14-16).<br>Halakha mentions other, rare kinds of fixed veset, such as 'veset ha-dilug' (the skipping veset), that is, instead of a fixed interval or a fixed date in the month, each month the cycle lengthens by a day or shortens by a day. And at times this cyclicity recurs alternately, and this is 'veset ha-sirug' (the alternating veset). This is the rule: whenever the cycle has appeared three times in a fixed cycle, she has become a woman with a fixed veset (SA 189:5, 7-8).<br>From the words of the Sages it is implied that most women have a fixed veset; however, nowadays only a minority have a fixed veset. It is possible that distance from life in nature has caused this. In addition, it is possible that there are women who have a veset ha-guf (as explained in section 7), but for lack of attentiveness to it they do not establish a veset ha-guf for themselves. And this is a pity, because after establishing a veset one must be concerned for one ona only, and not for three onot as is the law for a woman who has no fixed veset. In any case, I began in section 5 by explaining the law of a woman who has no fixed veset, both because this law pertains to most women today, and because even women who establish a veset – before they established the veset, their law is like that of women who have no fixed veset."},{"n":8,"en":"In Terumat Ha-deshen, section 247, he wrote that there is a semi-fixed veset, as explained above. Some are stringent that she is still obligated to separate also at the ona beinonit and at the onat ha-ḥodesh, and the novel point of the Terumat Ha-deshen is that although she has no fixed veset, and according to the Rambam and the Rosh she must examine herself before every union (as cited in note 1), since she has a fixed pattern of not seeing at certain times, at those times she need not examine herself before union. So wrote Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 8; Leḥem Ve-simla 8; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 20, and others. However, since the law of separation is rabbinic, the halakha follows the view of those who hold that she also need not separate at a time when she knows she will not see, among them: Malbushei Tahara 186:3; Taharat Yisrael 12; Igrot Moshe YD 2:72; Taharat Ha-bayit 3:8. And so wrote Darkhei Tahara 7:19, also regarding a fixed pattern before the thirtieth day.<br>A woman who had a semi-fixed veset and once saw on the thirtieth day should in the following month be concerned for the three onot of a veset that is not fixed. If she again sees in the manner of the semi-fixed veset, she has returned to her fixed pattern. And if three times she saw not in accordance with her semi-fixed pattern, her semi-fixed veset is entirely cancelled."}]},{"slug":"18-06-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-07/","title_he":"ז – וסת הגוף","title_en":"Veset Ha-guf","body_en":"<p>A woman who felt, shortly before the appearance of her cycle, a special physical sensation, such as pains in the abdomen, the back or the head, or a sensation of particular heaviness in one of her limbs, must be concerned for a veset ha-guf (bodily veset). That is, it is possible that these sensations are the distinct signs that the veset is about to arrive; then, in the following month, when she feels this, she should separate until the end of that ona. In addition, since she has not yet established the 'veset ha-guf', she must also be concerned for the three onot explained in section 5 (SA YD 189:19, 26). If she felt the special physical sensation again and her veset did not arrive, she need no longer be concerned for it.</p><p>If during three consecutive vestot the veset began after she felt that same sensation, she has established a veset ha-guf, and she need no longer be concerned for the other onot for which she was concerned in the past (Knesset Yeḥezkel 36; Darkhei Tahara 7:72-74). If, after she established the veset ha-guf, she felt her special sensation and the cycle did not arrive in the ona in which it was due to begin, in the following month she must separate both at the ona of the 'veset ha-guf' and at the onat ha-ḥodesh and the haflaga from the previous veset. And if three consecutive times pass in which she feels her special sensation and the veset does not arrive at its ona, her veset ha-guf is uprooted (SA 189:19-26).</p><p>There are women whose veset ha-guf heralds that the cycle will begin not in the current ona but in the one after it; then they must separate in the ona in which the veset is due to appear. At times the veset ha-guf heralds that the cycle will appear in another two or three days; then, if the ona of its appearance is distinct – a day ona or a night ona – one must separate in that ona only. And if the ona of its appearance is not distinct, but rather it will be in one of two onot, one must separate in both onot in which it may appear. But if the veset ha-guf does not point to one of two onot in which it will appear, but rather there is a chance that it will appear in one of three onot, this is not a veset ha-guf, and one need not be concerned for it.</p><p>The appearance of ketamim a few hours before the beginning of the veset can also be considered a veset ha-guf. That is, even though the ketamim themselves do not render impure, because they were on a colored garment and the like (above 2:7-9), since they contain a sign that the veset is about to arrive in that ona, when they appear again in the following month the couple must separate until the end of that ona. Usually these ketamim appear together with certain sensations, and then they are considered a veset ha-guf only when they appear with those same sensations. These sensations need not be defined; the main thing is that the woman knows that the ketem in combination with these sensations is a sign that the veset is about to appear.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"The signs of a veset ha-guf can also appear a day or several days before the appearance of the veset (Darkhei Teshuva 189:78; Igrot Moshe YD 3:51). When, according to the veset ha-guf, the woman knows that the veset will appear in another two or three days in one of two onot, I wrote that she should be concerned for both onot, in accordance with the approach of the Or Zaru'a. And although the halakha usually does not follow him (above, section 4, note 6), here, where in practice there is a reasonable concern that she will see in one of the two onot, it seems that one should take his approach into account. And perhaps this was his intent from the outset: that when there is a reasonable chance that she will see in one of two onot, she must be concerned for both of them. And it seems that this is the reason of the Aḥaronim who took his approach into account, who held that there is indeed a concern that the woman will see also one ona earlier.<br>It further seems that if, on the basis of the premenstrual ketamim, it is not possible to determine two onot in which the veset will appear, because the doubt is spread over three onot or more – even though these ketamim do not have the law of 'veset ha-guf' and onot of separation are not determined by them – since they herald the approach of the time of the veset, it is proper that from the time they appear, before they wish to have union, the woman should examine herself with an external bedika using toilet paper in the toilet, to verify that the veset has not begun."}]},{"slug":"18-06-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-08/","title_he":"ח – מדיני וסתות","title_en":"Some Laws of Vestot","body_en":"<p>The veset usually lasts several days, whether in a flow or in spurts that stop and resume; however, the ona of the veset is determined according to the time at which the veset begins (SA YD 184:6; Rema 189:13).</p><p>Vestot are established by blood that came out with sensation in the manner of a veset, and not by ketamim, even if they render impure (Ra'avad; SA 190:54).<sup>10</sup></p><p>A woman who was negligent in keeping the calculation of her ona of separation, and does not know when she must separate – whether she had a fixed veset or did not have a fixed veset – is permitted on all days. Because the obligation of separation at the ona close to the veset is from the words of the Sages, and in a doubt concerning the words of the Sages the halakha is to be lenient (Ḥavot Da'at 184:4; Minḥat Pitim; see Ḥatam Sofer YD 166). However, so that such a mishap not occur, every woman must keep an exact record of the time at which she gets her veset each month, and, if it appeared with special sensations, note those as well, so that she will know when she must separate, and also be able to know whether she has a fixed veset.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The view of most Rishonim is that the time determining the separation is the beginning of the veset, among them: Razah, Rashba, Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Meiri, Ritva and Ran. However, according to the Ra'avad and Ramban, the entire duration of the veset should be counted as onot in which one must be concerned and separate. And although Leḥem Ve-simla 21 and Ben Ish Ḥai, Tzav 4, took their view into account, in practice the halakha follows the view of most Rishonim, who establish separation for one ona according to the beginning of the veset. And so it was ruled in SA 184:6; Rema 189:13; Pardes Rimonim; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 32, and many others. (However, when the veset is short and lasts only around the hours of transition between two onot, one is concerned for the first ona and for the hours of the second ona during which it continues, SA 184:5.)<br>There are no vestot for ketamim; that is, when a woman became forbidden because of a ketem, she does not establish a veset according to the ketem with regard to the ona of separation (SA 190:54). Therefore, even if she saw the ketem through which she became forbidden several days before the beginning of the veset, her ona of separation is determined according to the seeing of veset blood that came out with sensation (Tzitz Eliezer part 6, 21:2; Shevet Ha-levi 3:118). And even if she became impure through an internal bedika at the end of the ona of separation, if the menstrual bleeding began in a different ona, the onot of separation in the following month are calculated from the time the menstrual bleeding began. But if it happened three times that she became forbidden through an internal bedika, for example in the bedika at the end of the ona of separation, since she has thereby established a presumption, from then on she should calculate the onot of separation from the ona of the seeing in the internal bedika (Harei Besamim 1:98; Pitḥa Zuta 190:102; Minḥat Yitzḥak 4:118:3; Taharat Ha-bayit 1, p. 75; and others. This is unlike Beit Shlomo 2:7 and Igrot Moshe YD 2:68, who hold that even after one time that blood was found in an internal bedika, the onot of separation should be determined according to it)."}]},{"slug":"18-06-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-09/","title_he":"ט – היוצא לדרך","title_en":"One Who Sets Out on a Journey","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva for a man who must travel away from home for several days to \"visit\" his wife, that is, to be with her, on the night before his journey. For the mitzva of ona requires fulfilling union at the fixed times, and in addition at a time when the desire of one of the spouses intensifies. By the nature of the world, before the husband is about to travel away from home their desire intensifies, and therefore they are obligated to have union on the night before his journey, so that they part with love and joy. And so is the law when the wife must travel away from home for several days.</p><p>The Sages said that by virtue of union on the night before the journey, they will merit to keep faith with one another and will not sin during their separation in thought or in deed of betrayal, as it is stated (Iyov 5:24): \"And you shall know that your tent is at peace, and you shall visit your habitation and shall not sin.\" Since this mitzva is great and important, the Sages said that it is a mitzva to have union even if the night before the husband's departure falls in the ona in which they must separate. For as long as she has not actually felt that the veset has begun, by Torah law they are permitted, and it was the Sages who established that it is a mitzva to separate at the ona of the veset; and since the obligation to have union before the journey is from the Torah, it overrides the mitzva of separation at the ona of the veset, which is from the words of the Sages.</p><p>However, some of the Rishonim were stringent and ruled that if the night before the journey falls in the ona of separation, union is forbidden, and the mitzva is rather to increase in words of affection and love (Rabbeinu Tam and the Rosh). But according to most poskim, even if the night before the journey falls in the ona of separation, they should have union. However, when possible, it is better to set the time of departure not in the ona of separation (Maharshal, Torat Ha-shelamim). When the journey is short, so that he will manage to return before the time of their mitzva of ona according to their regular practice arrives, they should part with a hug and a kiss and not with union.</p><p>This mitzva is unique to one who sets out on a journey, for it involves both a great obligation of the mitzva of ona and also shalom bayit (marital harmony) and protection from stumbling; but at other times when it is a mitzva to have union, even on the night of tevila, union in the ona of separation is not to be permitted.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Sages said in Yevamot 62b: \"Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: A man is obligated to visit his wife when he sets out on a journey, as it is stated: 'And you shall know that your tent is at peace,' etc. Is it derived from here? It is derived from there: 'And your desire shall be to your husband' – this teaches that a woman desires her husband when he sets out on a journey. Rav Yosef said: It was necessary only close to her veset… And this applies to an optional matter, but for a matter of mitzva – they are preoccupied.\" The Rishonim explained that even at an ona when they must separate, as long as she has not seen blood, it is a mitzva for one who sets out on a journey to visit his wife with union (Rashi, Ra'avad, Ra'avan, Ramban, Rashba, Ra'ah, Ran, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). And the Ritva added (Shevu'ot 18b) that the verse promises that she will not see blood at the time of union. On the other hand, according to Rabbeinu Tam, union at the ona of the veset is not to be permitted for this, and according to his reading the intent is to instruct that if his wife is a nidda (a menstruant woman) or at the ona of her fixed veset, he should part from her with words of affection. And so ruled the Razah, Rosh, Or Zaru'a and Teruma. In practice, in SA YD 184:10 it was ruled that it is obligatory to visit with actual union. And the Rema too agreed that this is the main halakha, but wrote, based on the Semag, that one who is stringent and makes do with all kinds of closeness that are not union, may a blessing come upon him. The Maharshal wondered what stringency there is in this, since according to most poskim it is obligatory to have union; how is one who refrains from it considered stringent? He therefore explained that the view of the Semag is that the stringent person should change the time of his departure in a way that he will be able to have union according to all views. So too wrote Torat Ha-shelamim 19. One should add that it seems that Rabbeinu Tam is stringent only at an ona of separation for a fixed veset, but for a veset that is not fixed he was not stringent (Merḥeshet 33:9; Taharat Ha-bayit part 1, p. 98, and others).<br>I wrote that also when the wife sets out on a journey it is a mitzva to have union (as explained in Peninei Halakha: Simḥat Ha-bayit U-virkhato 2:9); however, when the departure is in an ona of separation, there is room for doubt, for perhaps when the husband sets out there is more concern of stumbling (see Darkhei Teshuva 184:46; Shi'urei Shevet Ha-levi 1). On the other hand, we find that in practice there is reason for concern about stumbling also when the wife sets out. In practice, those who wish to have union, according to what they know of themselves, may do so.<br>One who sets out on a journey for a length of time such that he will manage to return before their next time of union arrives is not obligated to visit his wife, and consequently he may not have union in the ona of separation (Hagahot Ya'avetz, Yevamot 62b; Torat Ḥayim Sofer OḤ 240:4).<br>One who sets out for the sake of a mitzva, such as redeeming captives, and there is a concern that if he has union his mind will be diverted from the mitzva and he will not fulfill it properly, should not have union, for one who is engaged in a mitzva is exempt from another mitzva (Yevamot 62b, according to Rashi and Nimukei Yosef). But if there is no concern that the union will cause negligence in the mitzva, the obligation of the mitzva to have union returns to its place (Darkhei Tahara 6:17).<br>In Knesset Yeḥezkel 33 he permitted union also in an ona of separation that fell on the night of tevila; however, many disagreed with him, because this permission is unique to one who sets out on a journey. So wrote Pleti, Noda Bi-Yehuda, Sidrei Tahara, Ḥavot Da'at and others. However, regarding a bride whose ona of separation falls on her wedding night, some permit (Ha-mikneh; Shem Aryeh YD 43), and many forbid (Noda Bi-Yehuda, Mahadura Tinyana YD 117; R. Shlomo Kluger; Ben Ish Ḥai, Tzav 6)."}]},{"slug":"18-06-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-10/","title_he":"י – מעוברות ומיניקות – מסולקות דמים","title_en":"Pregnant and Nursing Women – Mesulakot Damim","body_en":"<p>A woman who has become pregnant, as long as she has not verified this by a reliable test, must during the first three months separate at the onot of her veset like all women. But if she knows with certainty, on the basis of a pregnancy test, that she is pregnant, she is considered 'mesuleket damim' (one whose blood has ceased), and she need no longer separate at the ona of her veset. And although at times she still sees blood and becomes impure from it, this is not according to the cycle of the veset but due to bleeding that sometimes occurs at the beginning of pregnancy.<sup>12</sup></p><p>Likewise, a nursing woman is considered 'mesuleket damim' as long as she nurses even once a day, provided that she has not yet seen her first veset and twenty-four months have not passed since the birth. But if she stopped nursing completely, or saw a veset, or twenty-four months have passed even though she is still nursing, she is not considered 'mesuleket damim', and she reverts to her previous laws. That is, if before the beginning of the pregnancy she had a fixed veset on the 3rd of the month, she should separate on the 3rd of the following month according to the law of her fixed veset. Likewise, if she had a 'veset ha-guf', when she again feels the sensation of her veset she should separate until the end of the ona, as she was accustomed. Likewise, if she was accustomed to see a fixed veset of intervals, after she sees once again she should return to observing her fixed interval veset. And if three times she did not see at the ona of her fixed veset, she is no longer concerned for it. And if she did not have a fixed veset, after she sees once she should be concerned for the three onot, in the manner of a woman who has no fixed veset.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"In the Mishna in Nidda 7a-b, the law of four women who do not usually see blood is explained (a pregnant woman, a nursing woman, a minor and an elderly woman): since they are 'mesulakot damim', if they saw blood, 'their time suffices for them', that is, they render teruma and taharot impure only from the time they saw the blood, and not retroactively for the twenty-four hours before, like other women. From here we also learned the law of separation at the ona of the veset: that they are considered mesulakot damim and need not separate.<br>Regarding a pregnant woman, it is explained in Nidda 8b: \"From when her fetus is recognizable. And how long until the fetus is recognizable? Sumkhus says in the name of R. Meir: Three months.\" And so it was ruled in SA YD 189:33. Some hold that even today, when it is possible to know with certainty through a test whether pregnancy has begun, a woman who had a fixed veset must be concerned for its onot until the end of the three months; and if she did not have a fixed veset, after one month in which she does not see, she no longer has onot in which she must separate (Taharat Ha-bayit 2:7; Badei Ha-shulḥan 184:7, Bi'urim). However, since in practice pregnancy stops the cycle of the vestot, and the bleeding that sometimes occurs is due to other causes, such as detachment of the placenta, one should therefore say that she is concerned for three months only when there was no certainty that the pregnancy had begun, but if there is certainty, she need not be concerned. So wrote Igrot Moshe YD 3:52; and Rav Elyakim Levanon in 'Shalom Ohalekha' 20:4.<br>A pregnant woman who saw blood and became impure: some say that she must separate at the onat ha-ḥodesh and the haflaga in the following month (SA 189:33; Levush 33; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 83; Ḥokhmat Adam 112:38, and others). And some say that only if she saw three times during her pregnancy will she need to separate at the ona of the veset (Maharshal; Derisha 184:16; Shakh 19, and others). And so is the halakha, since the dispute concerns a rabbinic law; and in addition, we know that there is no cyclicity in bleeding during pregnancy."},{"n":13,"en":"It is explained in Nidda 9a that according to R. Meir a nursing woman is considered 'mesuleket damim' as long as she nurses, whereas according to the Sages, for all twenty-four months from the birth, because in childbirth her limbs come apart, and only after twenty-four months does the woman who gave birth finish recovering. And so it was ruled in SA YD 189:33. However, nowadays (perhaps as a result of improved nutrition), even before twenty-four months have passed, the vestot return after nursing ends, and some even see regular vestot during nursing. Therefore, a woman who has stopped nursing is not to be regarded as mesuleket damim, and likewise a nursing woman who saw a veset is not to be regarded as mesuleket damim, as explained above. So wrote Igrot Moshe YD 3:52; Ḥut Shani 189:18. (According to Ḥut Shani 189:18 and those who agree with him, a woman who before the pregnancy had a fixed veset should, after the end of 24 months, again be concerned three times for that veset. However, in halakhic practice one need not be concerned for this, since in practice, when the vestot began before 24 months, we have not found that there is any difference between before 24 months and after them.)"}]},{"slug":"18-06-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-11/","title_he":"יא – דין הנוטלת גלולות הורמונליות","title_en":"The Law of a Woman Who Takes Hormonal Pills","body_en":"<p>A woman who takes hormonal pills that postpone the appearance of the veset, as long as she is taking the pills and according to the medical assessment she is not supposed to get a period, need not be concerned for the onot of separation, whether these are onot of separation due to a fixed veset or due to a veset that is not fixed.</p><p>After she stops taking the pills, she should separate at the ona in which she is due to get the veset. And since there are different pills, and their effect on women differs, each woman must check how long after she stops taking these pills she begins to see blood, and until she knows this, she should separate starting 48 hours after taking the last pill. Afterwards, if three times she finds that she begins to see blood after a longer time, for example three days from taking the last pill, she should establish a veset accordingly, and be concerned for the ona in which she is liable to get her period. And if she is in doubt, she should be stringent also for one ona earlier.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"Nidda 39a, in the Mishna: \"R. Meir says: If she was in hiding, and the time of her veset arrived and she did not examine, she is pure, because fear drives away the blood.\" R. Meir holds that vestot are from the Torah, and nevertheless he is lenient in a situation of fear; all the more so would those who hold that vestot are rabbinic be lenient in this. That is, she need not separate at the ona of her veset, nor examine herself at its end. So wrote the Rashba, and so it is implied by SA YD 184:8. However, according to the Ramban, le-khatḥila she must separate and examine at the end of the ona, and so wrote the Rema. And all this concerns fear, which does not with certainty halt the cycle; but when it concerns pills that have been scientifically tested, one may rely on them that they postpone the veset (Darkhei Tahara 7:101; Minḥat Shlomo Tinyana 75:1. This is unlike Levushei Oz 8:5 and Mishmeret Ha-tahara 7:99, who were lenient only after it had been proven for this woman that the pills postponed her veset three times).<br>As long as the woman does not know how long after taking the last pill the veset will come, she should separate starting 48 hours from taking it. And in Darkhei Tahara 7:102 he wrote that she should continue her separation until the end of seven days, for this is the time by which the veset may be delayed after taking the pills."}]},{"slug":"18-06-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-06-12/","title_he":"יב – מבוגרת","title_en":"An Older Woman","body_en":"<p>A woman who has reached menopause, at which the veset stops appearing, and ninety days have passed without her seeing a veset, is no longer subject to the law of separation at the ona of her veset. Because even if she sees a veset, since it is no longer regular, there is no time at which it is likely that she will see the next veset. However, when she sees blood she becomes impure like all women, but she will not need to calculate from that seeing onot in which they must separate.</p><p>If, after she stopped seeing for ninety days, she again saw three vestot in a fixed cycle, she has established a fixed veset (SA YD 189:30-31). And if the veset again appeared according to the previous fixed pattern she had, even once – for example, she was accustomed to see on every 3rd of the month and again saw on the 3rd of the month – immediately after one time she must again be concerned for her fixed pattern according to the law of a fixed veset. For it has become clear that that ninety-day stop was not the stop that heralded the final cessation of the cycle of the veset.</p><p>If, after she stopped seeing for ninety days, she again saw three vestot, each month or at most every two months, without a fixed cycle, from the appearance of the third cycle she should begin to be concerned for her seeings according to the law of a veset that is not fixed.<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"Nidda 9a-b: \"Who is an elderly woman? Any woman for whom three onot [here, three thirty-day periods] have passed [close to her old age]. What is 'close to her old age'? Rav Yehuda said: Any woman about whom her friends say, 'She is old' (but to her face they do not yet say this, so as not to embarrass her). And Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish says: Any woman whom they call 'Mother, Mother' and she is not embarrassed\" (they used to call an elderly woman 'Mother', and this is a later stage, at which she is no longer embarrassed that they call her 'Mother', as everyone used to call elderly women). And so it was ruled in SA YD 189:28-29. Today these criteria are not accepted in society, and therefore we cannot determine the law according to them. And some wished to fix an exact age for the definition of an elderly woman (see Otzar Ha-halakha 189:140). However, in practice the age at which the vestot end varies greatly from woman to woman, and therefore the Sages set the criterion according to what was evident to the woman's friends from the totality of her conduct, her vitality and her appearance, and not according to age. In practice, today women can know their hormonal state through the medical tests performed from time to time at the health funds; consequently, any woman who understands that she has reached menopause, in which the vestot become irregular, and for ninety days has not seen a veset, need no longer calculate onot of separation.<br>If she again sees three times, once a month or at most every two months, without a fixed cycle, after the third time it has become clear that her veset has not ceased, and since she has not established a veset, she must be concerned for her seeings according to the law of a veset that is not fixed (Taz 189:43; Sidrei Tahara 34; Ḥavot Da'at 35; Suga Ba-shoshanim). This is unlike those who hold that as long as she has not established a veset, even after three times she is not concerned for a veset that is not fixed (Shakh, Nekudot Ha-kesef 189:11; Ḥokhmat Adam 112:37). And after she again does not see for ninety days, she reverts to the law of mesuleket damim, who need not be concerned for times of separation on account of her seeings."}]}]});
